1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:01,877
So, thanks for coming by, guys.

2
00:00:01,919 --> 00:00:02,753
We just wanted to bounce

3
00:00:02,795 --> 00:00:03,754
some ideas off of you

4
00:00:03,796 --> 00:00:05,088
about the wedding ceremony.

5
00:00:05,130 --> 00:00:06,757
Oh...

6
00:00:06,799 --> 00:00:08,091
What's the matter?
Are you okay?

7
00:00:08,133 --> 00:00:10,010
Yeah, it's just, you know...

8
00:00:10,051 --> 00:00:11,470
Monica said, "Wedding."

9
00:00:11,512 --> 00:00:14,181
MONICA:
Uh, so, we wanted to
know if one of you

10
00:00:14,222 --> 00:00:16,600
would like to read something
at the ceremony.

11
00:00:16,642 --> 00:00:18,018
Okay, yeah

12
00:00:18,059 --> 00:00:21,229
I guess...
I guess I could do that, too.

13
00:00:21,271 --> 00:00:22,230
"Too"?

14
00:00:22,272 --> 00:00:24,441
Yeah, I kind of, uh

15
00:00:24,483 --> 00:00:27,486
( clearing throat ):
have something else planned
for you guys.

16
00:00:27,528 --> 00:00:29,613
Oh. Do you mind telling us
what it is?

17
00:00:29,655 --> 00:00:33,951
Sorry, I'm kind of keeping
this one on the QT.

18
00:00:33,993 --> 00:00:37,663
Well, whatever it is,
I hope it involves winking.

19
00:00:38,956 --> 00:00:39,915
Hey!

20
00:00:39,957 --> 00:00:41,542
OTHERS:
Hey.

21
00:00:41,583 --> 00:00:43,084
I just talked
to one of the DOOL writers...

22
00:00:43,126 --> 00:00:44,169
What is DOOL?

23
00:00:44,211 --> 00:00:45,379
Days of our Lives.

24
00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:46,630
Oh.

25
00:00:46,672 --> 00:00:48,924
Anyway, you're not going
to believe it--

26
00:00:48,966 --> 00:00:51,218
my character is coming
out of his coma!

27
00:00:51,259 --> 00:00:52,219
Hey, that's great!

28
00:00:52,260 --> 00:00:53,220
That's great!
Joey!

29
00:00:53,261 --> 00:00:54,555
And-And-And
not only that

30
00:00:54,596 --> 00:00:58,058
I'm getting a new brain!

31
00:00:58,099 --> 00:01:00,602
So, great things are
happening at work

32
00:01:00,644 --> 00:01:02,604
and in your
personal life.

33
00:01:02,646 --> 00:01:04,105
RACHEL:
Wait.

34
00:01:04,147 --> 00:01:05,357
What do you mean
you're getting a new brain?

35
00:01:05,399 --> 00:01:06,984
Well-Well,
they're killing off

36
00:01:07,026 --> 00:01:08,736
one of the characters
on the show

37
00:01:08,777 --> 00:01:11,488
and when she dies,
her brain is being transplanted

38
00:01:11,530 --> 00:01:12,489
into my body.

39
00:01:12,531 --> 00:01:13,741
Wh-What?

40
00:01:13,782 --> 00:01:16,785
A brain transplant?

41
00:01:16,827 --> 00:01:18,579
It's ridiculous!

42
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:20,247
Well, I think
it's ridiculous

43
00:01:20,288 --> 00:01:23,375
that you haven't had sex
in three and a half months.

44
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:27,546
It's winter.

45
00:01:27,588 --> 00:01:30,591
There are fewer people
on the street.

46
00:01:31,508 --> 00:01:33,218
Who are they killing off?

47
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:34,344
Uh, Cecilia Monroe.

48
00:01:34,386 --> 00:01:35,930
She plays
Jessica Lockhart.

49
00:01:35,971 --> 00:01:37,890
No...
No...

50
00:01:37,932 --> 00:01:39,058
She is so good

51
00:01:39,098 --> 00:01:40,851
at throwing drinks
in people's faces.

52
00:01:40,893 --> 00:01:42,227
I mean, I don't think

53
00:01:42,269 --> 00:01:43,938
I've ever seen her finish
a beverage.

54
00:01:43,979 --> 00:01:44,980
MONICA:
And the way

55
00:01:45,022 --> 00:01:46,231
she slaps people
all the time.

56
00:01:46,273 --> 00:01:47,858
Wouldn't you love
to do that just once?

57
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:50,652
Don't do it.

58
00:01:50,694 --> 00:01:52,195
And she's been
on the show forever.

59
00:01:52,237 --> 00:01:54,322
It's going to be really hard
to fill her shoes.

60
00:01:54,364 --> 00:01:56,992
Yeah, yeah.
Help me out here.

61
00:01:57,034 --> 00:02:02,081
When you... when
you come out of the
"brain transplant"

62
00:02:02,122 --> 00:02:04,249
you are going
to be her?

63
00:02:04,291 --> 00:02:07,461
Yes, but in
Drake Ramoray's body.

64
00:02:07,502 --> 00:02:09,964
( mumbles incredulously )

65
00:02:10,005 --> 00:02:11,131
Why is it so hard
for you to get?

66
00:02:11,172 --> 00:02:13,008
I thought you were
a scientist.

67
00:02:20,223 --> 00:02:25,186
♪ So no one told you
life was gonna be this way ♪

68
00:02:25,228 --> 00:02:27,648
♪ Your job's a joke,
you're broke ♪

69
00:02:27,689 --> 00:02:29,984
♪ Your love life's D.O.A. ♪

70
00:02:30,025 --> 00:02:34,655
♪ It's like you're always
stuck in second gear ♪

71
00:02:34,696 --> 00:02:36,949
♪ When it hasn't been
your day, your week ♪

72
00:02:36,991 --> 00:02:40,243
♪ Your month,
or even your year ♪

73
00:02:40,285 --> 00:02:43,538
♪ But I'll be there for you ♪

74
00:02:43,580 --> 00:02:49,628
♪ 'Cause you're there
for me, too. ♪

75
00:02:52,798 --> 00:02:54,716
Rach, so,
that guy there...

76
00:02:54,758 --> 00:02:55,968
straight or gay?

77
00:02:57,052 --> 00:02:58,720
RACHEL:
Oh...

78
00:02:58,762 --> 00:03:01,723
( laughing ):
Oh, yeah, he's too
cute to be straight.

79
00:03:01,765 --> 00:03:04,559
Ooh, Knockers will help us
figure it out.

80
00:03:04,601 --> 00:03:05,602
Oh.

81
00:03:09,606 --> 00:03:12,693
All right,
straight...

82
00:03:12,734 --> 00:03:15,320
and not subtle.

83
00:03:17,322 --> 00:03:19,449
Ooh, he left
his cell phone.
Oh.

84
00:03:19,491 --> 00:03:20,784
Well, we can give
it to Gunther

85
00:03:20,826 --> 00:03:22,327
and he'll put it
in lost and found.

86
00:03:22,369 --> 00:03:24,412
Or we could use it
to call China

87
00:03:24,454 --> 00:03:26,289
see how those guys
are doing.

88
00:03:26,331 --> 00:03:30,335
What if, um, if he calls
his own cell phone
to find out who found it

89
00:03:30,377 --> 00:03:32,295
and I answer and
we start talking

90
00:03:32,337 --> 00:03:33,547
and we fell in love?

91
00:03:33,588 --> 00:03:35,883
I mean, wouldn't that
be a great story?

92
00:03:35,924 --> 00:03:38,677
Kind of like a fairy tale
for the digital age.

93
00:03:38,719 --> 00:03:40,679
Yeah...
that does sound great.

94
00:03:40,721 --> 00:03:42,097
I'm going
to get the phone.

95
00:03:42,139 --> 00:03:43,222
What? Wait!

96
00:03:43,264 --> 00:03:45,142
Wh-Why... why do
you get the story?

97
00:03:45,184 --> 00:03:46,393
I don't know.

98
00:03:46,434 --> 00:03:48,520
I haven't been
out on a date in so long.

99
00:03:48,562 --> 00:03:51,272
Phoebe, you had a
date three days ago.

100
00:03:51,314 --> 00:03:52,399
That wasn't a date.

101
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:53,901
That was...
that was just friends

102
00:03:53,942 --> 00:03:55,485
getting together...
having sex.

103
00:03:55,527 --> 00:03:57,320
Okay. Great.

104
00:03:57,362 --> 00:03:58,363
See? I get the phone.

105
00:03:58,405 --> 00:03:59,907
No way.
No way.

106
00:03:59,948 --> 00:04:01,825
You just broke up with Tag
a week ago.

107
00:04:01,867 --> 00:04:03,744
Yeah, and until now...

108
00:04:03,785 --> 00:04:07,330
( voice breaking ):
I didn't think
I would love again.

109
00:04:07,372 --> 00:04:08,832
Nice try.

110
00:04:08,874 --> 00:04:10,250
Well, hey, wait, wait.

111
00:04:10,291 --> 00:04:13,003
How do we fairly decide
who gets the phone?

112
00:04:13,045 --> 00:04:15,463
I don't know.

113
00:04:19,634 --> 00:04:21,511
Well, um...

114
00:04:21,553 --> 00:04:25,348
maybe we
could... uh...

115
00:04:25,390 --> 00:04:26,808
Aha, too slow!

116
00:04:26,850 --> 00:04:28,810
( laughing cockily )

117
00:04:28,852 --> 00:04:29,978
Aha!

118
00:04:30,020 --> 00:04:31,730
Too cocky.

119
00:04:35,483 --> 00:04:37,027
I'm going to keep
dating him, Mother

120
00:04:37,069 --> 00:04:38,820
and there's nothing
you can do about it.

121
00:04:38,862 --> 00:04:40,114
Oh, yes, there is.

122
00:04:40,155 --> 00:04:41,907
What are you going to do,
kill him

123
00:04:41,949 --> 00:04:43,158
like you did Charles?

124
00:04:44,993 --> 00:04:47,621
Oh, my baby!

125
00:04:47,662 --> 00:04:48,663
( sobs once )

126
00:04:51,374 --> 00:04:52,876
MAN:
Cut.

127
00:04:52,918 --> 00:04:54,920
( scattered applause )

128
00:04:57,005 --> 00:04:58,966
That was a great scene.

129
00:04:59,007 --> 00:05:01,009
An-And that slap
looked so real.

130
00:05:01,051 --> 00:05:02,301
How do you do that?

131
00:05:02,343 --> 00:05:04,512
Oh, just years
of experience.

132
00:05:04,554 --> 00:05:06,431
( crying ):
Can I get
some ice here?

133
00:05:06,473 --> 00:05:08,683
Well, anyway,
I just wanted

134
00:05:08,725 --> 00:05:10,936
to say how wonderful
I think you are.

135
00:05:10,978 --> 00:05:13,521
You're not the fan
who's dying, are you?

136
00:05:13,563 --> 00:05:14,522
Uh, no.

137
00:05:14,564 --> 00:05:15,774
No, I'm Joey
Tribbiani.

138
00:05:15,816 --> 00:05:17,692
We did a scene
together yesterday.

139
00:05:17,734 --> 00:05:19,069
I'm the guy
in the coma.

140
00:05:19,111 --> 00:05:21,696
Oh, that was
a real person?

141
00:05:21,738 --> 00:05:23,031
( stammers softly )

142
00:05:23,073 --> 00:05:24,699
A-A-Anyway, I just
wanted to say

143
00:05:24,741 --> 00:05:27,077
that since I'm getting
your brain when
you leave the show

144
00:05:27,119 --> 00:05:28,870
I was wondering if
there's any tips...

145
00:05:28,912 --> 00:05:31,372
Wait. I-I'm leaving the show?

146
00:05:31,414 --> 00:05:33,917
I don't know. Why?
Did you hear something.

147
00:05:35,585 --> 00:05:37,045
Who told you that?

148
00:05:37,087 --> 00:05:39,173
Uh... uh...
one of the writers.

149
00:05:39,214 --> 00:05:40,174
Which writer?

150
00:05:40,215 --> 00:05:41,549
Was it bald
or was it tall?

151
00:05:41,591 --> 00:05:43,135
Uh, you know, uh...
You know what?

152
00:05:43,177 --> 00:05:45,679
It doesn't matter,
because it is not true.

153
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:47,139
Okay.

154
00:05:47,181 --> 00:05:49,224
And if it were true,
how dare you come to me

155
00:05:49,266 --> 00:05:51,059
and ask me for tips
about a character

156
00:05:51,101 --> 00:05:52,811
that I've been playing
for 20 years.

157
00:05:52,853 --> 00:05:53,895
I'll give you a tip.

158
00:05:59,026 --> 00:06:00,319
Miss Monroe...

159
00:06:00,359 --> 00:06:01,862
Oh, there you go.

160
00:06:06,241 --> 00:06:07,701
Okay, look,
I have an idea.

161
00:06:07,742 --> 00:06:09,536
Why don't we...
why don't we see

162
00:06:09,577 --> 00:06:12,164
what kind of numbers he
has on his speed dial?

163
00:06:12,206 --> 00:06:15,083
And, then, from that,
we can tell who has
more in common with him

164
00:06:15,125 --> 00:06:16,835
and, then, whoever
does, gets the phone.

165
00:06:16,877 --> 00:06:18,253
Fine. All right.

166
00:06:18,295 --> 00:06:19,587
All right.

167
00:06:19,629 --> 00:06:22,883
First name on the
speed dial is "Mom."

168
00:06:22,924 --> 00:06:23,884
Oh...

169
00:06:23,925 --> 00:06:26,553
I lost my mom
to suicide.

170
00:06:26,594 --> 00:06:28,013
Okay, no way.

171
00:06:28,055 --> 00:06:29,639
You cannot use that
to get the cute guy

172
00:06:29,681 --> 00:06:31,016
and the last
blueberry muffin.

173
00:06:31,058 --> 00:06:33,018
Did I use that
already today?
Yes.

174
00:06:33,060 --> 00:06:34,477
I'm sorry.
Okay.

175
00:06:34,519 --> 00:06:37,064
"Ben... Carlos...

176
00:06:37,105 --> 00:06:38,148
David..."

177
00:06:38,190 --> 00:06:39,316
Oh, I win.

178
00:06:39,358 --> 00:06:42,361
He's got Barney's
on his speed dial.

179
00:06:42,401 --> 00:06:44,779
So, you don't know
that it's Barney's, the store.

180
00:06:44,821 --> 00:06:47,490
That could be, you know,
his friend's house or a bar.

181
00:06:47,532 --> 00:06:50,285
Who has Barney's, the store,
on their speed dial?

182
00:06:50,327 --> 00:06:53,205
His new girlfriend.

183
00:06:57,416 --> 00:07:00,670
( bagpipes droning dissonantly)

184
00:07:06,134 --> 00:07:07,928
What is that?

185
00:07:07,969 --> 00:07:10,847
I think it's the
Dying Cat Parade.

186
00:07:12,807 --> 00:07:15,769
It sounds like it's coming
from across the street.

187
00:07:15,810 --> 00:07:18,105
Oh, my God.

188
00:07:18,146 --> 00:07:19,898
What?

189
00:07:19,940 --> 00:07:21,900
You know that thing
that Ross was going
to do at our wedding?

190
00:07:21,942 --> 00:07:23,360
He was hanging out
with me yesterday

191
00:07:23,402 --> 00:07:24,652
and he turned to
me and he said

192
00:07:24,694 --> 00:07:26,321
"You're half-
Scottish, right?"

193
00:07:26,363 --> 00:07:28,240
No. There is no way...

194
00:07:28,282 --> 00:07:29,866
It cannot be Ross.

195
00:07:37,665 --> 00:07:39,293
Unbelievable!

196
00:07:39,334 --> 00:07:41,544
Why is your family
Scottish?

197
00:07:41,586 --> 00:07:45,006
Why is your family Ross?

198
00:07:45,048 --> 00:07:48,176
He cannot play
at our wedding.

199
00:07:48,218 --> 00:07:50,304
I mean,
everyone will leave.

200
00:07:50,345 --> 00:07:53,181
I mean, come on,
that is just noise!

201
00:07:53,223 --> 00:07:55,434
It's not even a song!

202
00:07:55,474 --> 00:07:58,979
If you listen
very carefully,
I think...

203
00:07:59,020 --> 00:08:01,898
it's "Celebration"
by Kool and the Gang.

204
00:08:01,940 --> 00:08:03,317
( bagpipes continue )

205
00:08:08,529 --> 00:08:09,530
Well, you're
absolutely right.

206
00:08:09,572 --> 00:08:11,366
They are writing me
out of the show.

207
00:08:11,408 --> 00:08:13,618
I don't know exactly
when it's going to happen

208
00:08:13,660 --> 00:08:15,662
but apparently it's
going to be very soon

209
00:08:15,703 --> 00:08:16,788
and then that's... it.

210
00:08:16,830 --> 00:08:17,789
I'm so sorry.

211
00:08:17,831 --> 00:08:18,873
Look, if it was
up to me

212
00:08:18,915 --> 00:08:20,208
you would never
leave the show.

213
00:08:20,250 --> 00:08:21,209
Yeah, thanks.

214
00:08:21,251 --> 00:08:22,461
No, I mean it.

215
00:08:22,501 --> 00:08:24,671
I can't believe they would
do this to you

216
00:08:24,712 --> 00:08:26,173
and to your fans.

217
00:08:26,214 --> 00:08:29,384
I mean, they're going
to be devastated, heartbroken.

218
00:08:29,426 --> 00:08:31,011
They love you so much.

219
00:08:31,052 --> 00:08:33,347
Oh, you're right.

220
00:08:33,388 --> 00:08:34,514
Thank you.

221
00:08:34,555 --> 00:08:36,183
What's your name again?

222
00:08:36,224 --> 00:08:38,559
Joey.

223
00:08:38,601 --> 00:08:40,687
Joey. Well, thank you.

224
00:08:40,728 --> 00:08:42,605
That is so sweet.

225
00:08:42,647 --> 00:08:44,399
Lo-lo-lo-look,
the only reason

226
00:08:44,441 --> 00:08:46,693
I came up to you before
was because...

227
00:08:46,734 --> 00:08:50,071
well, because I'm really nervous
about-about being you.

228
00:08:50,113 --> 00:08:51,739
You know,
if you could

229
00:08:51,781 --> 00:08:55,369
help me capture the-the essence
of the character, you know?

230
00:08:55,410 --> 00:08:57,745
Help me keep Jessica alive.
Please?

231
00:08:59,789 --> 00:09:02,542
All right, Joey,
I will help you...

232
00:09:02,583 --> 00:09:05,962
not because I-I owe it
to this stupid show

233
00:09:06,004 --> 00:09:08,798
but because...
I owe it to Jessica.

234
00:09:08,840 --> 00:09:10,842
Oh, that's great.
Oh, thank you so much.

235
00:09:10,884 --> 00:09:12,260
You're so welcome.

236
00:09:12,302 --> 00:09:14,637
Now, I've been watching
some tapes. How's this?

237
00:09:14,679 --> 00:09:18,892
( with British accent):
Jessica Lockhart will never
step foot in this place again!

238
00:09:18,933 --> 00:09:21,353
Ever!

239
00:09:21,395 --> 00:09:26,024
Well, but... Jessica doesn't
have an English accent.

240
00:09:26,066 --> 00:09:29,361
I can do an English accent?

241
00:09:29,403 --> 00:09:33,156
That baby's going
on my resume.

242
00:09:35,700 --> 00:09:36,826
( knocking )

243
00:09:36,868 --> 00:09:37,827
Hi, Pheebs.

244
00:09:37,869 --> 00:09:39,079
How are you?
Good.

245
00:09:39,120 --> 00:09:40,914
Uh, Pheeb,
remember when we were

246
00:09:40,955 --> 00:09:42,790
in the coffeehouse,
we decided

247
00:09:42,832 --> 00:09:45,919
that I was going
to keep the cute
guy's cell phone?

248
00:09:45,960 --> 00:09:46,836
Yeah.

249
00:09:46,878 --> 00:09:47,837
And remember how I said

250
00:09:47,879 --> 00:09:49,130
I would keep it in my purse

251
00:09:49,172 --> 00:09:50,715
so that if it rang
I could just pick it up?

252
00:09:50,757 --> 00:09:51,758
Yeah!
Right?

253
00:09:51,799 --> 00:09:53,427
Um, and, then,
do you remember

254
00:09:53,468 --> 00:09:56,137
going into my purse
and stealing the phone?

255
00:09:56,179 --> 00:09:58,973
Ooh, now you lost me.

256
00:09:59,015 --> 00:10:00,559
You stole the phone!

257
00:10:00,600 --> 00:10:01,851
No, I didn't!

258
00:10:01,893 --> 00:10:03,644
No? So you're saying
that if I called it...

259
00:10:03,686 --> 00:10:04,812
it wouldn't ring?

260
00:10:04,854 --> 00:10:05,813
Nope.

261
00:10:05,855 --> 00:10:07,232
Mmm. Okay.

262
00:10:07,274 --> 00:10:08,900
But while you dial,
let me show you the features

263
00:10:08,942 --> 00:10:10,026
of my new ringing handbag.

264
00:10:10,068 --> 00:10:11,652
Mmm.

265
00:10:11,694 --> 00:10:12,653
( phone ringing )

266
00:10:12,695 --> 00:10:13,821
Oh, it does work!

267
00:10:13,863 --> 00:10:16,575
Oh, Phoebe!

268
00:10:16,616 --> 00:10:18,034
That is a different phone.

269
00:10:18,076 --> 00:10:19,494
Oh, is it?

270
00:10:19,536 --> 00:10:20,995
Uh, hello.

271
00:10:21,037 --> 00:10:22,872
Yes, hi, is Rachel there?

272
00:10:22,914 --> 00:10:24,916
Yes, she is. Just
one moment please.

273
00:10:24,958 --> 00:10:26,667
It's for me!

274
00:10:26,709 --> 00:10:30,505
Yeah, that is damning evidence.

275
00:10:30,547 --> 00:10:31,798
( phone ringing )

276
00:10:31,839 --> 00:10:33,674
Oh, my God!

277
00:10:33,716 --> 00:10:35,676
I bet that's him.

278
00:10:35,718 --> 00:10:38,221
My digital fairy tale
is about to begin.

279
00:10:40,265 --> 00:10:42,850
Would you stop doing that?

280
00:10:42,892 --> 00:10:44,644
Hello.

281
00:10:44,685 --> 00:10:46,521
Yes, I'm the one
who found your phone.

282
00:10:46,563 --> 00:10:47,606
Phoebe, you can't...!

283
00:10:47,688 --> 00:10:49,107
I'm on a call.

284
00:10:49,149 --> 00:10:51,109
Oh...

285
00:10:51,151 --> 00:10:53,445
I mean, yeah,
you could pick it up tonight

286
00:10:53,487 --> 00:10:56,990
at, say, 8:30...
at... at my apartment.

287
00:10:57,031 --> 00:11:01,995
It's, um, it's, um,
5 Morton Street, Apartment 14.

288
00:11:02,036 --> 00:11:03,871
And then, maybe,
you know, after

289
00:11:03,913 --> 00:11:06,291
we could grab
a bite to eat or whatever.

290
00:11:06,333 --> 00:11:09,085
Okay, well, okay,
I'll see you then. Bye.

291
00:11:10,753 --> 00:11:14,048
You do know that I will
be here when he comes over.

292
00:11:14,090 --> 00:11:16,426
Oh, and how will you know
what time to come over?

293
00:11:16,468 --> 00:11:18,970
You just said it.

294
00:11:21,764 --> 00:11:23,224
Oh.

295
00:11:26,227 --> 00:11:29,564
The essence
of the character

296
00:11:29,606 --> 00:11:32,233
is rooted in
her confidence.

297
00:11:32,275 --> 00:11:35,278
So, when Jessica enters
a room, for instance

298
00:11:35,320 --> 00:11:37,947
she owns everything

299
00:11:37,989 --> 00:11:42,076
and every person
in that room.

300
00:11:42,118 --> 00:11:43,077
You try it.

301
00:11:43,119 --> 00:11:44,287
Okay.

302
00:11:52,795 --> 00:11:55,048
Right. Well, he's
not mad at the room.

303
00:11:55,089 --> 00:11:57,091
Try it again.

304
00:11:57,133 --> 00:11:58,092
He owns it.

305
00:11:58,134 --> 00:11:59,427
He owns the room.

306
00:11:59,469 --> 00:12:00,595
It is his.

307
00:12:00,637 --> 00:12:02,430
He owns, owns,
owns the room!

308
00:12:02,472 --> 00:12:04,765
He owns it!

309
00:12:04,807 --> 00:12:08,727
All right, it's a
little weird, but
it's getting better.

310
00:12:08,769 --> 00:12:11,105
Oh, I'm going to miss
this woman so much.

311
00:12:11,147 --> 00:12:13,149
I-I don't know what
I'm going to do.

312
00:12:13,191 --> 00:12:15,402
I mean, it's been
20 years of my life.

313
00:12:15,443 --> 00:12:16,403
Oh, then...

314
00:12:16,444 --> 00:12:17,404
Hey, hey!

315
00:12:17,445 --> 00:12:18,572
Maybe... maybe, uh

316
00:12:18,613 --> 00:12:20,448
maybe this is a good
thing, you know?

317
00:12:20,490 --> 00:12:22,950
It'll give you a chance
to shake things up...

318
00:12:22,992 --> 00:12:24,494
play different
characters.

319
00:12:24,536 --> 00:12:25,744
You're so talented.

320
00:12:25,786 --> 00:12:28,122
I probably should've
just left years ago

321
00:12:28,164 --> 00:12:30,250
when the offers
were pouring in

322
00:12:30,291 --> 00:12:33,420
but, you know, I just got
so comfy here and...

323
00:12:33,461 --> 00:12:36,631
Oh, I turned down
some amazing work.

324
00:12:36,673 --> 00:12:37,965
Like what?

325
00:12:38,007 --> 00:12:39,133
Well...

326
00:12:39,175 --> 00:12:41,969
let's just say
if I left 15 years ago

327
00:12:42,011 --> 00:12:46,849
the landscape of Mexican cinema
would be very different today.

328
00:12:48,685 --> 00:12:51,271
Wow.

329
00:12:51,312 --> 00:12:53,814
But... well, now...

330
00:12:53,856 --> 00:12:56,317
now is a different time
for me.

331
00:12:58,319 --> 00:12:59,487
Oh, hey, come on.

332
00:12:59,529 --> 00:13:01,197
Don't... don't do this.

333
00:13:01,239 --> 00:13:04,409
Um... Look, let me tell
you something, okay?

334
00:13:04,451 --> 00:13:06,661
Now, when I watch
you do a scene

335
00:13:06,703 --> 00:13:10,123
I'm thinking, "Boy,
she-she is a great actress"

336
00:13:10,164 --> 00:13:14,711
but... but... I'm also
thinking, "She is hot!"

337
00:13:16,921 --> 00:13:19,716
You think I'm hot?

338
00:13:19,758 --> 00:13:22,719
You own the room.

339
00:13:26,055 --> 00:13:29,058
We should probably
get... get, uh...

340
00:13:29,100 --> 00:13:30,059
Oh, yeah, yeah.

341
00:13:30,101 --> 00:13:31,227
We should get...

342
00:13:35,398 --> 00:13:37,358
So, when Jessica...

343
00:13:37,400 --> 00:13:40,361
kisses a man...

344
00:13:40,403 --> 00:13:45,366
she usually puts, um...
both her hands on the...

345
00:13:45,408 --> 00:13:46,701
man's face.

346
00:13:46,743 --> 00:13:47,826
Yeah, I noticed that.

347
00:13:47,868 --> 00:13:49,370
Is that 'cause she's
so passionate?

348
00:13:49,412 --> 00:13:52,039
No, it's because that way
the camera only sees her.

349
00:13:52,081 --> 00:13:54,292
Oh.

350
00:13:56,461 --> 00:13:58,755
You want to try it?

351
00:13:58,797 --> 00:14:00,465
Yeah. Okay.

352
00:14:12,477 --> 00:14:13,812
That was good.

353
00:14:13,852 --> 00:14:16,063
That was really good...

354
00:14:16,105 --> 00:14:18,817
but I-I think
that maybe your hands are

355
00:14:18,857 --> 00:14:20,193
just a little off.

356
00:14:20,234 --> 00:14:23,363
They should be, maybe,
like, right... right...

357
00:14:23,404 --> 00:14:25,198
( grunting )

358
00:14:30,911 --> 00:14:33,872
Well, I feel like a snack.

359
00:14:33,914 --> 00:14:35,750
Do you want
some shortbread?

360
00:14:35,792 --> 00:14:37,669
It's Scottish,
like you are.

361
00:14:37,711 --> 00:14:39,212
Oh, no, thanks.

362
00:14:39,253 --> 00:14:42,382
I don't like anything
from my Scottish heritage.

363
00:14:42,423 --> 00:14:44,133
What?

364
00:14:44,175 --> 00:14:46,344
Well, it's just
that my entire family

365
00:14:46,386 --> 00:14:49,597
was run out of
Scotland by...

366
00:14:49,639 --> 00:14:51,516
Vikings.

367
00:14:51,558 --> 00:14:54,769
Oh, it sounds to me
like your family is ready

368
00:14:54,811 --> 00:14:57,230
to, uh, rediscover
its Scottish roots.

369
00:14:57,271 --> 00:15:00,775
You can't play bagpipes
at the wedding.

370
00:15:00,817 --> 00:15:02,777
How did you know about that?

371
00:15:02,819 --> 00:15:05,279
We heard you play
all the way from
your apartment.

372
00:15:05,321 --> 00:15:07,824
Were you the ones
who called the cops?

373
00:15:07,866 --> 00:15:10,951
That's not really
important right now.

374
00:15:10,993 --> 00:15:12,620
( clears throat )

375
00:15:12,662 --> 00:15:15,456
What is important is while
we appreciate the gesture

376
00:15:15,498 --> 00:15:19,502
we just don't feel that bagpipes
are appropriate for our wedding.

377
00:15:19,544 --> 00:15:20,503
Why not?

378
00:15:20,545 --> 00:15:22,296
Because we hate them.

379
00:15:22,338 --> 00:15:25,007
Just-Just give me a
chance to perform for you

380
00:15:25,049 --> 00:15:27,301
and then decide
whatever you want...

381
00:15:27,343 --> 00:15:31,806
and I'm not going to
tell you what song I'm
going to play, either

382
00:15:31,848 --> 00:15:34,475
but, uh, let's just say,
when it's over

383
00:15:34,517 --> 00:15:37,771
I'll bet there'll be a
wee bit o' celebration.

384
00:15:44,235 --> 00:15:46,945
Hey! Hey!

385
00:15:46,987 --> 00:15:49,824
( knocking on door)
BOTH:
Who is it?

386
00:15:49,866 --> 00:15:53,620
Hi. It's Tom. I'm here
to pick up the phone.

387
00:15:53,661 --> 00:15:56,163
Well, why do you get
to answer the door?

388
00:15:56,205 --> 00:15:57,998
Well, why shouldn't I?

389
00:15:58,040 --> 00:15:59,500
'Cause it's my apartment.

390
00:15:59,542 --> 00:16:01,836
Well, then I get to give him
the cell phone.

391
00:16:01,878 --> 00:16:04,714
Okay. Good luck explaining
all the calls to China.

392
00:16:07,926 --> 00:16:09,719
Hi.

393
00:16:13,431 --> 00:16:16,851
Wow, how long
were we arguing for?

394
00:16:17,894 --> 00:16:19,520
Um...

395
00:16:19,562 --> 00:16:20,979
( chuckles )

396
00:16:21,021 --> 00:16:22,899
You're not the man who
left the cell phone.

397
00:16:22,941 --> 00:16:24,233
No. That's my assistant.

398
00:16:24,275 --> 00:16:26,861
Is-is he coming?

399
00:16:26,903 --> 00:16:28,279
Uh... no.

400
00:16:28,321 --> 00:16:31,324
Could you... could you,
uh, give us one second?

401
00:16:31,365 --> 00:16:33,367
Sure.
We'll we be
right back, sir.

402
00:16:34,869 --> 00:16:37,037
Well, what do we do?

403
00:16:37,079 --> 00:16:39,081
I don't know.

404
00:16:39,123 --> 00:16:40,500
Can you believe that?

405
00:16:40,541 --> 00:16:42,042
We're waiting for a hot guy

406
00:16:42,084 --> 00:16:44,044
and then an even
hotter one shows up.

407
00:16:44,086 --> 00:16:45,254
I know.

408
00:16:49,258 --> 00:16:50,468
What?

409
00:16:50,510 --> 00:16:52,303
Rachel, listen...

410
00:16:52,345 --> 00:16:54,138
if you let me have him

411
00:16:54,180 --> 00:16:56,766
then I will really
owe you one.

412
00:16:58,184 --> 00:17:02,062
All right.

413
00:17:02,104 --> 00:17:04,816
All right, Phoebe,
I will let you have him

414
00:17:04,858 --> 00:17:07,485
but you owe me--
you owe me big.

415
00:17:07,527 --> 00:17:09,028
So, which one of
you lovely ladies

416
00:17:09,069 --> 00:17:10,613
am I going to take
to dinner, huh?

417
00:17:10,655 --> 00:17:13,031
Oh, that'd be me... sir.

418
00:17:13,073 --> 00:17:14,408
After you.

419
00:17:14,450 --> 00:17:16,703
Okay, thank you.
Okay.

420
00:17:17,620 --> 00:17:18,997
( whispering ):
Nice.

421
00:17:23,125 --> 00:17:27,922
Well, you certainly
owned that room.

422
00:17:29,841 --> 00:17:31,592
Actually, I rent
the whole place

423
00:17:31,634 --> 00:17:34,094
and I... just got what
you meant. Thank you.

424
00:17:34,136 --> 00:17:35,638
( laughing )

425
00:17:37,139 --> 00:17:39,767
Hi.
JOEY:
Hey.

426
00:17:39,809 --> 00:17:41,811
Oh, my God.

427
00:17:41,853 --> 00:17:43,897
Oh, my God!

428
00:17:43,938 --> 00:17:46,106
Oh! Jessica Lockhart!

429
00:17:46,148 --> 00:17:48,442
In my apartment!

430
00:17:48,484 --> 00:17:51,946
I am such a huge fan.
I am such a huge fan.

431
00:17:51,988 --> 00:17:53,489
Well, it's nice
to know that you...

432
00:17:53,531 --> 00:17:56,283
Monica! Monica!

433
00:17:56,325 --> 00:17:58,411
That, uh, that's
my roommate Rachel.

434
00:17:58,452 --> 00:18:01,039
Oh, that explains all
the women's underwear.

435
00:18:01,080 --> 00:18:03,374
Sure.

436
00:18:03,416 --> 00:18:07,211
Oh, my God, it's true!

437
00:18:08,629 --> 00:18:12,842
Oh, my God. Can I just ask you
to do me... just one favor?

438
00:18:12,884 --> 00:18:14,510
Certainly.

439
00:18:14,552 --> 00:18:15,970
Would you slap me?

440
00:18:16,012 --> 00:18:17,722
Would you slap me
right here in the face?

441
00:18:17,764 --> 00:18:22,351
I'd love to, but my lawyer
said I can't do that anymore.

442
00:18:24,812 --> 00:18:25,980
All right, here we go.

443
00:18:26,022 --> 00:18:27,607
All right,
just one more thing.

444
00:18:27,648 --> 00:18:29,400
One-one more thing. Um...

445
00:18:30,317 --> 00:18:32,070
You're a stupid bitch.

446
00:18:32,110 --> 00:18:33,696
I really can't slap you.
Oh!

447
00:18:33,738 --> 00:18:34,864
JOEY:
Here you go.

448
00:18:34,906 --> 00:18:36,074
My God, you're great!

449
00:18:36,115 --> 00:18:37,366
Thanks for stopping by.

450
00:18:37,408 --> 00:18:39,869
See you.

451
00:18:39,911 --> 00:18:41,746
I-I am so sorry.

452
00:18:41,788 --> 00:18:43,873
Oh, no-no-no-no.
Being adored-- I'm used to it.

453
00:18:43,915 --> 00:18:45,041
Don't worry about it.

454
00:18:45,083 --> 00:18:48,586
Oh, my God.
What?

455
00:18:48,628 --> 00:18:50,088
They sent me today's script.

456
00:18:50,129 --> 00:18:51,756
They never send me the script.

457
00:18:51,798 --> 00:18:52,799
They don't?

458
00:18:52,840 --> 00:18:54,550
Well, no. I'm just in a coma.

459
00:18:54,592 --> 00:18:56,343
This must mean I have lines.

460
00:18:58,096 --> 00:18:59,305
Oh.

461
00:18:59,346 --> 00:19:03,601
How does it happen?

462
00:19:05,352 --> 00:19:10,900
Ooh. You get thrown
from a horse into
an electric fence.

463
00:19:10,942 --> 00:19:12,026
( gasps )

464
00:19:12,068 --> 00:19:13,193
What?

465
00:19:13,235 --> 00:19:14,528
Jessica hates horses.

466
00:19:14,570 --> 00:19:16,196
Yeah, well,
I'm guessing after this

467
00:19:16,238 --> 00:19:18,825
she's not going to be crazy
about electricity, either.

468
00:19:18,866 --> 00:19:20,451
Oh...

469
00:19:23,121 --> 00:19:27,583
Loosening the saddle
on Mother's horse was
brilliant, Frederick.

470
00:19:27,625 --> 00:19:30,210
And the electric
fence, inspired.

471
00:19:30,252 --> 00:19:32,379
Thank you, sweetheart.

472
00:19:32,421 --> 00:19:35,883
I can't believe
she's really gone.

473
00:19:35,925 --> 00:19:38,427
Look around you--
all of this is ours.

474
00:19:38,469 --> 00:19:40,471
I don't think so.

475
00:19:45,476 --> 00:19:46,811
Who are you?

476
00:19:46,853 --> 00:19:48,813
What's the matter, Dina?

477
00:19:48,855 --> 00:19:51,816
Don't you recognize your own...

478
00:19:51,858 --> 00:19:53,693
mother?

479
00:19:55,903 --> 00:19:58,865
MAN:
Cut. That was great,
everybody. Thank you.

480
00:19:58,906 --> 00:20:01,450
That was so wonderful!

481
00:20:01,492 --> 00:20:04,787
Oh, I think that you're a better
Jessica than I ever was.

482
00:20:04,829 --> 00:20:06,372
Oh, no.

483
00:20:06,413 --> 00:20:09,583
Well, of course not,
but you were very good.
Thanks.

484
00:20:09,625 --> 00:20:12,252
And guess what? Good news--
I got another job.

485
00:20:12,294 --> 00:20:13,629
Great! Hey, all right!

486
00:20:13,671 --> 00:20:14,839
Wha-What is it?

487
00:20:14,881 --> 00:20:17,383
A film in Guadalajara.

488
00:20:17,424 --> 00:20:20,636
The airport?

489
00:20:20,678 --> 00:20:23,597
No, that's La Guardia.

490
00:20:23,639 --> 00:20:26,600
Ah.

491
00:20:26,642 --> 00:20:28,978
This is... Mexico.

492
00:20:29,020 --> 00:20:30,396
Oh. Whoa.

493
00:20:30,437 --> 00:20:33,149
Well, how-how long
will you be gone?

494
00:20:33,191 --> 00:20:35,317
Eight months.

495
00:20:35,359 --> 00:20:37,695
That's a really long time.

496
00:20:37,737 --> 00:20:39,739
Yeah, but you could
come and visit me.

497
00:20:39,780 --> 00:20:44,284
I bet that you could, uh,
own a few places down there.

498
00:20:44,326 --> 00:20:49,040
You know, I'll tell you,
I should probably buy a
place in the city first.

499
00:20:50,457 --> 00:20:52,710
And I just got what
you meant again.

500
00:20:52,752 --> 00:20:54,128
That is a...
I'll tell you

501
00:20:54,170 --> 00:20:56,422
that's a tricky one.
It's a tricky one.

502
00:20:56,463 --> 00:20:58,591
Well...

503
00:20:59,592 --> 00:21:01,510
Good luck.

504
00:21:01,552 --> 00:21:03,679
You, too.

505
00:21:19,612 --> 00:21:21,030
Okay, now,
uh, remember...

506
00:21:21,072 --> 00:21:22,907
I'm still learning.

507
00:21:22,949 --> 00:21:25,910
( bagpipe squeaking
and groaning )

508
00:21:31,916 --> 00:21:34,710
One, two, three, four!

509
00:21:34,752 --> 00:21:38,756
( squeaky notes
over cacophonous groaning )

510
00:21:52,603 --> 00:21:55,564
You know the song--
sing along!

511
00:21:55,606 --> 00:21:57,357
( bagpipe resumes )

512
00:21:57,399 --> 00:22:01,320
( screeching "Celebration"
by Kool &amp; the Gang )

513
00:22:11,914 --> 00:22:13,958
So?

514
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,002
No.
No.

